$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/encrypted-folder
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  A Safekeys folder is the transport unit: an ordinary directory (or object prefix) holding
  ciphertext blobs, a manifest, and an agent-readable README with no secrets. Folders can be copied,
  synced, or passed between agents freely — copying increases no cryptographic exposure, and only a
  live token plus a reachable sidecar and key authority allow use.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/sidecar
status: built
intent: |
  Secrets need to move the way artefacts move — through git, object stores, artefacts folders, and
  agent handoffs — without each hop being a new exposure. Making the transport unit an inert
  ciphertext container means the risky part of the journey (copying) carries no risk at all, and the
  only sensitive operation (resolve) is gated by a token and a privileged process.
flows:
  - id: build-folder
    name: Build an encrypted folder
    description: Ciphertext objects plus a manifest are written to a directory or object prefix.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: generate
        target: For each secret, generate a random DEK and encrypt the plaintext into objects/<id>.enc.
      - id: s2
        action: generate
        target: >-
          Write manifest.json — object ids, content-type hints, token references, wrapping key ids,
          and hashes.
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: Write README.safekeys describing the folder for humans and agents, containing no secrets.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: The folder is now inert; it can be copied or committed without further protection.
  - id: copy-folder
    name: Transport a folder
    description: A folder is moved between environments or agents with no unwrap occurring.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Destination receives the folder or object prefix verbatim.
      - id: s2
        action: observe
        target: No unwrap is attempted; no key material is required to hold the copy.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: Cryptographic exposure is unchanged by the copy.
contracts:
  - id: folder-is-inert
    description: A folder on its own must be useless without a live token and a reachable key authority.
    must_have:
      - Only ciphertext objects, a manifest, and a no-secret README are present
      - No key material, DEK, or KEK is stored in the folder
      - Copying the folder does not increase cryptographic exposure
  - id: manifest-is-metadata-only
    description: The manifest describes structure and references but never values.
    must_have:
      - Object ids, content-type hints, token references, wrapping key ids, and hashes only
      - No plaintext, no recoverable key bytes
      - A manifest leaking entirely must not yield any secret
  - id: readme-is-agent-safe
    description: The README is deliberately readable by humans and models.
    must_have:
      - Explains what the folder is and how to use it
      - Contains no secrets and no key material
      - Safe to surface in an agent's context
tests:
  - id: folder-copy-yields-nothing
    flow: copy-folder
    setup:
      folder_with_secrets: true
    expect:
      - assertion: the copied folder contains no plaintext
      - assertion: reading every file in the folder without a sidecar yields no secret
    contracts:
      - folder-is-inert
  - id: manifest-leak-yields-nothing
    flow: build-folder
    setup:
      manifest_only: true
    expect:
      - assertion: no secret is recoverable from manifest.json alone
      - assertion: wrapping key ids are identifiers, not key material
    contracts:
      - manifest-is-metadata-only
  - id: readme-safe-for-model-context
    flow: build-folder
    expect:
      - assertion: README.safekeys contains no secret values
      - assertion: pasting README.safekeys into a model context exposes nothing sensitive
    contracts:
      - readme-is-agent-safe
decisions:
  - id: ordinary-directory
    decision: Use an ordinary directory or object prefix rather than a custom container format.
    rationale: >-
      Any filesystem, object store, or artefact system can carry it with no adapter, and tooling
      (diff, sync, tar) works unchanged. The security comes from the contents being ciphertext, not
      from the container.
    status: accepted
  - id: aes-gcm-or-chacha
    decision: Encrypt objects with AES-256-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305.
    rationale: >-
      Both are AEAD constructions with wide hardware acceleration and mature library support, giving
      confidentiality and integrity without bespoke crypto.
    status: accepted
  - id: readme-is-a-feature
    decision: Ship a README.safekeys in every folder, designed to be model-readable.
    rationale: >-
      Agents will encounter folders; a clear, secret-free explanation prevents an agent from
      attempting to extract values or misusing the folder, and keeps the folder self-describing in
      handoffs.
    status: accepted
  - id: manifest-is-metadata-only
    decision: >-
      The manifest may hold a wrapped DEK but never plaintext, an unwrapped DEK, or a KEK, enforced
      structurally via additionalProperties: false.
    rationale: >-
      Envelope-wrapped keys are useless without the KEK, which never leaves the key store, so
      permitting them lets the folder be a self-contained transport unit while granting nothing on
      its own. Forbidding everything else means a manifest can be leaked in full without exposing a
      secret. Schema-level rejection is stronger than a documented rule because it cannot be
      misconfigured.
    consequences: >-
      An implementation that encounters an unwrapped DEK in a manifest must refuse and log a
      security event, since it indicates a compromised writer. Conformance vectors assert the
      negative cases.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-22"
  - id: objects-path-constraint
    decision: Require every object path to match ^objects/[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*\.enc$.
    rationale: >-
      A path constraint prevents two failure modes at once: an unencrypted file masquerading as
      ciphertext, and path traversal escaping the folder. Both are structural, so the schema can
      reject them before any code runs.
    consequences: >-
      Folders must keep ciphertext strictly under objects/ with an .enc suffix. Enforced by the
      path-traversal and unencrypted-object-path conformance vectors.
    status: accepted
    date: "2026-09-22"
implementation:
  primary: pkg/protocol/folder.go
  test_code: pkg/protocol/protocol_test.go
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - smith-gray/envelope-encryption
  - smith-gray/secret-transport
  - smith-gray/capability-token
  - smith-gray/control-plane-db
